{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789843131,"nonce":"oXuvmrUO+6virlShO1ogSnQymkU=","title":"the entry file is compiled twice, and its statics got two homes","message":"The harness's injected `main` says `new TestDefaultArgs()`, so dependency discovery resolves `unit.TestDefaultArgs` on the class path to the entry file itself and compiles it as an import (import-order 41) before compiling it again as the entry. Every class in the file exists twice; a `private class Helper { static var counter }` had one global written by the import copy's `make()` and another read by the entry copy's test (`eq(2, Helper.counter)` read 0 while `make()` returned 41, 42).\n\nSkipping the import copy (canonical-path match against `user_files`) is NOT the fix: 16 rows regressed, because the entry compile alone does not see its own sibling types the way the import compile does (`Cannot find name 'C'`, `uncompiled unit.issues.TD.func`, class-not-registered on private siblings). The import copy has been providing forward declarations for every test's siblings.\n\nLanded instead: a static field whose `Class.field` global an earlier module already owns aliases that global (metadata.rs), and static reads/writes resolve by qualified name (`static_global_for`) because the second typing of the same class gives the field a second symbol. `global_type_of` supplies the type of an aliased global.\n\nOpen: make the entry compile self-sufficient (register a file's own declarations before lowering, as the import path does), then drop the duplicate compile.","files":null}]}